1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,505
Payday hits, and for about 48 hours,

2
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:04,519
you feel rich.

3
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:06,384
Then rent, subscriptions,

4
00:00:06,384 --> 00:00:09,273
and one little impulse order take everything.

5
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,892
And by the 20th, you're broke again.

6
00:00:12,152 --> 00:00:13,627
If that sounds familiar,

7
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:14,980
this video is for you.

8
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,127
Welcome to Broke to Balance,

9
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:20,310
where we turn paychecks into plans.

10
00:00:20,570 --> 00:00:20,959
Today,

11
00:00:20,959 --> 00:00:22,965
you'll learn how to budget your

12
00:00:22,965 --> 00:00:24,971
paycheck using one simple rule,

13
00:00:24,971 --> 00:00:26,394
in under five minutes.

14
00:00:27,794 --> 00:00:28,376
Step one:

15
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:31,154
budget your take-home pay, not your salary.

16
00:00:31,414 --> 00:00:33,726
Take-home is the money that actually

17
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:35,461
lands in your bank account,

18
00:00:35,461 --> 00:00:37,195
after taxes and deductions.

19
00:00:37,455 --> 00:00:41,283
In this example, you earn $3,600 a month.

20
00:00:41,543 --> 00:00:44,724
After $600 in taxes and deductions,

21
00:00:44,984 --> 00:00:47,597
$3,000 is yours to plan.

22
00:00:48,997 --> 00:00:51,034
Meet the 50/30/20 rule,

23
00:00:51,034 --> 00:00:54,753
popularized by Elizabeth Warren and Amelia

24
00:00:54,753 --> 00:00:58,295
Warren Tyagi in the book All Your Worth.

25
00:00:58,555 --> 00:01:01,478
50% of your take-home goes to needs.

26
00:01:01,738 --> 00:01:03,427
30% goes to wants.

27
00:01:03,687 --> 00:01:06,750
And 20% goes to savings and debt payoff.

28
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:11,828
On $3,000, that's $1,500, $900, and $600.

29
00:01:13,228 --> 00:01:15,647
Needs are the bills you can't skip:

30
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:18,617
rent, groceries, utilities, transportation,

31
00:01:18,617 --> 00:01:19,585
and insurance.

32
00:01:19,845 --> 00:01:21,878
Add them up in a spreadsheet, like this.

33
00:01:22,138 --> 00:01:26,031
In our example, needs total $1,500,

34
00:01:26,031 --> 00:01:27,366
exactly 50%.

35
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:29,925
If yours come in higher, don't panic.

36
00:01:29,925 --> 00:01:32,224
That's not failure, it's information,

37
00:01:32,224 --> 00:01:34,150
and it shows you where to trim.

38
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:37,286
Wants are what make life fun:

39
00:01:37,286 --> 00:01:39,860
eating out, streaming, shopping, and trips.

40
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,445
$900 is a real amount of fun,

41
00:01:42,445 --> 00:01:45,332
but only if you decide where it goes

42
00:01:45,332 --> 00:01:47,256
before the month starts.

43
00:01:47,516 --> 00:01:48,555
Here's the trick:

44
00:01:48,555 --> 00:01:50,693
give every want a name and a limit.

45
00:01:52,093 --> 00:01:55,064
The last 20% is what builds your balance.

46
00:01:55,324 --> 00:01:57,624
First, a starter emergency fund.

47
00:01:57,884 --> 00:01:59,914
Then, pay down high-interest debt.

48
00:02:00,174 --> 00:02:01,996
Then, invest for your future.

49
00:02:02,256 --> 00:02:07,766
Saving $600 a month adds up to $7,200 in one year.

50
00:02:08,026 --> 00:02:12,038
Invested for 5 years at an assumed 7%

51
00:02:12,038 --> 00:02:15,617
average return (not a guarantee),

52
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:18,979
it could grow to about $43,000.

53
00:02:19,239 --> 00:02:21,241
That's the power of consistency.

54
00:02:23,241 --> 00:02:24,917
Three tips to make this stick.

55
00:02:25,177 --> 00:02:25,440
One:

56
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,690
pay yourself first.

57
00:02:26,690 --> 00:02:28,663
Automate a transfer on payday,

58
00:02:28,663 --> 00:02:31,820
so your savings leave before you can spend them.

59
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:32,332
Two:

60
00:02:32,332 --> 00:02:34,412
track it in a simple spreadsheet.

61
00:02:34,412 --> 00:02:36,556
10 minutes a week is all it takes.

62
00:02:36,816 --> 00:02:37,278
Three:

63
00:02:37,278 --> 00:02:39,822
adjust the rule to fit your life.

64
00:02:39,822 --> 00:02:42,212
If rent is high where you live,

65
00:02:42,212 --> 00:02:43,214
try 60/20/20.

66
00:02:43,214 --> 00:02:45,218
Progress beats perfection.

67
00:02:46,618 --> 00:02:47,756
Before we wrap up,

68
00:02:47,756 --> 00:02:50,411
three mistakes that quietly break budgets.

69
00:02:50,671 --> 00:02:51,452
Mistake one:

70
00:02:51,452 --> 00:02:53,730
budgeting your gross salary instead

71
00:02:53,730 --> 00:02:55,162
of your take-home pay.

72
00:02:55,162 --> 00:02:56,985
That makes every number look

73
00:02:56,985 --> 00:02:58,612
bigger than it really is.

74
00:02:58,872 --> 00:02:59,688
Mistake two:

75
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:01,522
forgetting irregular costs,

76
00:03:01,522 --> 00:03:04,308
like car repairs, gifts, and annual fees.

77
00:03:04,308 --> 00:03:06,347
Set aside a little each month,

78
00:03:06,347 --> 00:03:08,181
so they don't surprise you.

79
00:03:08,441 --> 00:03:09,337
Mistake three:

80
00:03:09,337 --> 00:03:12,086
making your budget so strict that you quit.

81
00:03:12,086 --> 00:03:13,301
Leave room for fun,

82
00:03:13,301 --> 00:03:15,539
because a budget you can stick with

83
00:03:15,539 --> 00:03:17,457
beats a perfect one you can't.

84
00:03:18,857 --> 00:03:20,218
So, here's the recap.

85
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:22,120
50% for needs.

86
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:23,850
30% for wants.

87
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:25,943
20% for future you.

88
00:03:26,203 --> 00:03:27,101
If this helped,

89
00:03:27,101 --> 00:03:28,897
subscribe to Broke to Balance,

90
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:30,573
and tell me in the comments:

91
00:03:30,573 --> 00:03:32,488
which bucket is hardest for you?

92
00:03:32,748 --> 00:03:35,254
And if you learned something from this video,

93
00:03:35,254 --> 00:03:37,314
don't forget to watch our next video.

94
00:03:37,574 --> 00:03:39,986
This video is for education only,

95
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:41,959
and isn't financial advice.

96
00:03:41,959 --> 00:03:43,275
See you next time.

